[2026-01-30T00:36:17.590Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J3Nz9o7FdtUsCKE3SdQWML","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# ⚡ Claude Code Idea Generation: Performance\n     2→\n     3→## Mission\n     4→You are tasked with generating high-quality backlog ideas for the \"res\" project.\n     5→Your role is: **Performance**\n     6→\n     7→\n     8→## Target Context\n     9→- Context ID: ctx_1769727798577_zneo2y1\n    10→- Context Name: Report Theming & Cards\n    11→\n    12→\n    13→---\n    14→\n    15→## CRITICAL: Understanding Your Task\n    16→\n    17→**IMPORTANT DISTINCTION - READ CAREFULLY:**\n    18→\n    19→1. **ANALYZE**: The \"res\" project (the codebase you're exploring)\n    20→2. **SAVE TO**: Vibeman's idea management database (a SEPARATE system at http://localhost:3000)\n    21→\n    22→You are NOT creating API endpoints. You are NOT modifying the target project's code.\n    23→You are ANALYZING the target project and SAVING your findings to Vibeman's external API.\n    24→\n    25→The /api/scans and /api/ideas endpoints below are **Vibeman's management APIs** - they already exist.\n    26→Do NOT attempt to create these endpoints in the \"res\" project.\n    27→\n    28→Your job is:\n    29→- READ and ANALYZE the \"res\" codebase\n    30→- GENERATE ideas based on your analysis\n    31→- SAVE those ideas by calling Vibeman's existing APIs via curl\n    32→\n    33→---\n    34→\n    35→## Analysis Prompt\n    36→\n    37→Below is the specialized analysis prompt for this scan type. Use this to guide your analysis:\n    38→\n    39→---\n    40→\n    41→You are the **Performance Virtuoso** — a master of computational efficiency with unparalleled insight into a specific context within the \"res\" project.\n    42→\n    43→## Your Mastery\n    44→\n    45→You perceive time at the millisecond level. You understand that **speed is felt, not measured** — users don't see numbers, they feel responsiveness. You've optimized systems from the CPU cache to the CDN edge. You know that the fastest code is often the code that doesn't run at all.\n    46→\n    47→Your expertise spans the full stack: render cycles, database queries, network waterfalls, bundle sizes, memory patterns. You don't just find slow code — you understand *why* it's slow and *how* the architecture permitted it.\n    48→\n    49→## Your Creative License\n    50→\n    51→**Challenge assumptions about what's possible.** Performance optimization is not just about micro-optimizations — it's about **rethinking the approach entirely**. Consider:\n    52→\n    53→- What if we didn't need this operation at all?\n    54→- What if we did this work once instead of repeatedly?\n    55→- What if we moved this to the edge/client/server strategically?\n    56→- What if we predicted what the user needs before they ask?\n    57→\n    58→You have permission to propose architectural changes, not just tweaks. The biggest performance gains come from eliminating work, not optimizing it.\n    59→\n    60→## Performance Dimensions\n    61→\n    62→### ⚡ Computational Elegance\n    63→- **Algorithmic Alchemy**: O(n²) hiding in a loop. O(n) solutions that should be O(1).\n    64→- **Redundant Recalculation**: The same expensive operation happening multiple times per frame\n    65→- **Strategic Laziness**: Work being done eagerly that could be deferred or eliminated\n    66→- **Batching Blindness**: Individual operations that scream to be batched\n    67→\n    68→### 🎭 Render Intelligence\n    69→- **Cascade Prevention**: One change triggering unnecessary updates across the tree\n    70→- **Virtual Reality**: Large lists being fully rendered when only a viewport is visible\n    71→- **Memo Mastery**: Components that should remember themselves but don't\n    72→- **Layout Thrashing**: Reads and writes interleaved, forcing constant reflow\n    73→\n    74→### 🌐 Network Sorcery\n    75→- **Waterfall Elimination**: Sequential requests that could be parallel or combined\n    76→- **Payload Precision**: Sending megabytes when kilobytes would do\n    77→- **Prefetch Prophecy**: Data the user will need in 3 seconds, fetched now\n    78→- **Cache Consciousness**: The same data fetched repeatedly when it could be stored\n    79→\n    80→### 🧠 Memory Wisdom\n    81→- **Leak Archaeology**: References held long after their usefulness expired\n    82→- **Garbage Generation**: Temporary objects created in hot paths, churning the GC\n    83→- **Structure Efficiency**: Data shaped for convenience instead of access patterns\n    84→\n    85→### 🎪 Perceived Performance\n    86→- **Optimistic Illusions**: Show success immediately, confirm later\n    87→- **Progressive Revelation**: Show something fast, enhance progressively\n    88→- **Skeleton Strategies**: Structure appears instantly, content follows\n    89→- **Anticipatory Actions**: Start loading what they'll likely click\n    90→\n    91→## CRITICAL: JSON Output Format\n    92→\n    93→**You MUST respond with ONLY a valid JSON array. Follow these rules EXACTLY:**\n    94→\n    95→1. ❌ NO markdown code blocks (no ```json or ```)\n    96→2. ❌ NO explanatory text before or after the JSON\n    97→3. ❌ NO comments in the JSON\n    98→4. ✅ ONLY pure JSON array starting with [ and ending with ]\n    99→\n   100→**Expected JSON structure (copy this structure exactly):**\n   101→\n   102→[\n   103→  {\n   104→    \"category\": \"functionality\",\n   105→    \"title\": \"Short, descriptive title (max 60 characters)\",\n   106→    \"description\": \"Detailed explanation of the idea, what it solves, and how it helps (2-4 sentences). Be specific about implementation approach.\",\n   107→    \"reasoning\": \"Why this idea is valuable. What problem does it solve? What's the impact? (2-3 sentences).\",\n   108→    \"effort\": 2,\n   109→    \"impact\": 3\n   110→  }\n   111→]\n   112→\n   113→### Field Requirements:\n   114→\n   115→**REQUIRED FIELDS** (must be present in every idea):\n   116→- `title`: string (max 60 chars, clear and specific)\n   117→- `category`: string (one of the valid categories for your scan type)\n   118→- `description`: string (2-4 sentences, implementation-focused)\n   119→- `reasoning`: string (2-3 sentences, value-focused)\n   120→\n   121→**STRONGLY RECOMMENDED FIELDS** (should always be included):\n   122→- `effort`: number (1, 2, or 3 - implementation difficulty)\n   123→- `impact`: number (1, 2, or 3 - value to project)\n   124→\n   125→### Effort and Impact Ratings:\n   126→\n   127→**Effort** (Implementation difficulty):\n   128→- **1** = Low effort (Quick fix, minor change, 1-2 hours)\n   129→- **2** = Medium effort (Moderate change, requires planning, 1-2 days)\n   130→- **3** = High effort (Major change, significant refactoring, 1+ weeks)\n   131→\n   132→**Impact** (Value to project):\n   133→- **1** = Low impact (Nice to have, minor improvement)\n   134→- **2** = Medium impact (Noticeable improvement, good value)\n   135→- **3** = High impact (Game changer, major value, critical improvement)\n   136→\n   137→### Valid Categories:\n   138→- `functionality`: New features, missing capabilities, workflow improvements\n   139→- `performance`: Speed, efficiency, memory, database, rendering optimizations\n   140→- `maintenance`: Code organization, refactoring, technical debt, testing\n   141→- `ui`: Visual design, UX improvements, accessibility, responsiveness\n   142→- `code_quality`: Security, error handling, type safety, edge cases\n   143→- `user_benefit`: High-level value propositions, business impact, user experience\n   144→\n   145→---\n   146→\n   147→### Valid Categories for This Scan:\n   148→- **performance**: Speed optimizations, efficiency improvements, resource management\n   149→- **ui**: User experience, visual design, accessibility, responsiveness\n   150→\n   151→### Your Standards:\n   152→1.  **Measured Impact**: \"This runs 1000x per page load\" beats \"this could be faster\"\n   153→2.  **Root Cause**: Identify the architectural reason, not just the symptom\n   154→3.  **Trade-off Transparent**: What do we sacrifice? Readability? Complexity?\n   155→4.  **Implementation Path**: Specific techniques, libraries, patterns to apply\n   156→\n   157→---\n   158→\n   159→\n   160→\n   161→## Context Information\n   162→\n   163→**Context Name**: Report Theming & Cards\n   164→\n   165→**Context Description**:\n   166→Themed UI components for reports: stat cards, sections, badges, and core elements. Includes animated numbers and theme context for consistent styling.\n   167→\n   168→**Files in this Context** (9 files):\n   169→- src/components/report/ThemedCards.tsx\n   170→- src/components/report/core/ThemeContext.tsx\n   171→- src/components/report/core/AnimatedNumber.tsx\n   172→- src/components/report/core/index.tsx\n   173→- src/components/report/shared/Cards.tsx\n   174→- src/components/report/shared/Badges.tsx\n   175→- src/components/report/shared/ProgressBar.tsx\n   176→- src/components/report/shared/Icons.tsx\n   177→- src/components/report/shared/index.tsx\n   178→\n   179→\n   180→\n   181→## Existing Ideas\n   182→\n   183→No pending or accepted ideas found. This is a fresh analysis.\n   184→\n   185→\n   186→\n   187→\n   188→\n   189→---\n   190→\n   191→## Your Investigation\n   192→\n   193→1.  **Identify Hot Paths**: What runs frequently? What runs on user interaction?\n   194→2.  **Trace Data Flow**: Where does data come from? How many transformations?\n   195→3.  **Question Necessity**: For each operation, ask \"must this happen here, now, this way?\"\n   196→4.  **Think in Frames**: Would this cause jank? Would the user feel the delay?\n   197→\n   198→### Pursue:\n   199→- Eliminations over optimizations (the best optimization is removal)\n   200→- Caching, memoization, and computed derivations\n   201→- Lazy loading, code splitting, and progressive enhancement\n   202→- Database query optimization and indexing strategies\n   203→- Bundle analysis and tree-shaking opportunities\n   204→\n   205→### Avoid:\n   206→- Premature optimization of rarely-executed code\n   207→- Micro-optimizations that sacrifice readability for nanoseconds\n   208→- Performance work without considering the user experience impact\n   209→- Generic advice without connection to actual code patterns\n   210→\n   211→### Expected Output:\n   212→Generate 3-5 **TRANSFORMATIVE** performance ideas. Focus on changes that users will *feel* — faster page loads, smoother interactions, instant feedback. We want ideas that make the app feel like it's reading the user's mind.\n   213→\n   214→\n   215→**Performance Deep Dive**:\n   216→The context described above is your optimization target.\n   217→- What operations here are on the critical path?\n   218→- What's the heaviest computation happening?\n   219→- How does this perform at 10x, 100x scale?\n   220→- What would make this feel instant?\n   221→\n   222→\n   223→\n   224→---\n   225→\n   226→## ⚠️ FINAL REMINDER: OUTPUT FORMAT\n   227→\n   228→Your response must be ONLY a JSON array. Here's what your response should look like:\n   229→\n   230→[{\"category\":\"functionality\",\"title\":\"Add user profile caching\",\"description\":\"Implement Redis caching for user profile data to reduce database queries. Cache should invalidate on profile updates and have a 5-minute TTL. This will significantly reduce load on the users table.\",\"reasoning\":\"User profiles are accessed on every page load but rarely change. Caching reduces DB load by ~80% and improves page load times. High impact for minimal implementation effort.\",\"effort\":1,\"impact\":3}]\n   231→\n   232→❌ DO NOT wrap in markdown:\n   233→```json\n   234→[...]\n   235→```\n   236→\n   237→❌ DO NOT add explanations:\n   238→Here are the ideas:\n   239→[...]\n   240→\n   241→✅ ONLY output the JSON array, nothing else. Generate as many high-quality ideas as you believe would genuinely push this project to the next level - focus on quality and actionability over quantity.\n   242→\n   243→\n   244→## Project Goals\n   245→\n   246→No open goals found for this project.\n   247→\n   248→\n   249→\n   250→---\n   251→\n   252→## Saving Ideas to Vibeman's Database\n   253→\n   254→**Note:** These API calls go to Vibeman's idea management system (http://localhost:3000), NOT the project you're analyzing.\n   255→\n   256→You need to perform TWO steps to save ideas:\n   257→\n   258→### Step 1: Create a Scan Record\n   259→First, create a scan record to track this idea generation session.\n   260→\n   261→```bash\n   262→curl -s -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/scans \\\n   263→  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n   264→  -d '{\n   265→    \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n   266→    \"scan_type\": \"claude_code_perf_optimizer\",\n   267→    \"summary\": \"Claude Code idea generation - Performance\"\n   268→  }'\n   269→```\n   270→\n   271→The response will include a `scan.id` - save this for the next step.\n   272→\n   273→### Step 2: Create Ideas\n   274→For each idea, make a POST request with this JSON body:\n   275→\n   276→```\n   277→POST http://localhost:3000/api/ideas\n   278→Content-Type: application/json\n   279→\n   280→{\n   281→  \"scan_id\": \"<scan_id_from_step_1>\",\n   282→  \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n   283→  \"context_id\": \"ctx_1769727798577_zneo2y1\",\n   284→  \"scan_type\": \"perf_optimizer\",\n   285→  \"category\": \"<category>\",\n   286→  \"title\": \"<title>\",\n   287→  \"description\": \"<description>\",\n   288→  \"reasoning\": \"<reasoning>\",\n   289→  \"effort\": <1-10>,\n   290→  \"impact\": <1-10>,\n   291→  \"risk\": <1-10>,\n   292→  \"goal_id\": \"<goal_id_if_matched>\"\n   293→}\n   294→```\n   295→\n   296→**IMPORTANT:** Always include effort, impact, and risk scores (1-10) for every idea. Do NOT leave these fields empty or null.\n   297→\n   298→### Field Requirements\n   299→\n   300→**category** (string): One of:\n   301→- `functionality`: New features, missing capabilities, workflow improvements\n   302→- `performance`: Speed, efficiency, memory, database, rendering optimizations\n   303→- `maintenance`: Code organization, refactoring, technical debt, testing\n   304→- `ui`: Visual design, UX improvements, responsiveness\n   305→- `code_quality`: Security, error handling, type safety, edge cases\n   306→- `user_benefit`: High-level value propositions, business impact, user experience\n   307→\n   308→**title** (string, max 60 chars): Clear, specific, action-oriented title\n   309→\n   310→**description** (string): 2-4 sentences explaining:\n   311→- What the idea is\n   312→- How it would be implemented\n   313→- What problem it solves\n   314→\n   315→**reasoning** (string): 2-3 sentences explaining:\n   316→- Why this idea is valuable\n   317→- What impact it will have\n   318→- Why now is a good time to implement it\n   319→\n   320→**effort** (number 1-10) - Total cost to deliver: time, complexity, people, and coordination overhead:\n   321→- 1-2 = Trivial (few hours to a day, single file/config change, no coordination)\n   322→- 3-4 = Small (few days, localized to one module, minimal testing)\n   323→- 5-6 = Medium (1-2 weeks, multiple components, requires thoughtful testing)\n   324→- 7-8 = Large (several weeks to a month, spans multiple services, requires coordination)\n   325→- 9-10 = Massive (multi-month initiative, dedicated team, new architecture)\n   326→\n   327→**impact** (number 1-10) - Business value, user satisfaction, and strategic alignment:\n   328→- 1-2 = Negligible (nice-to-have, no measurable user/business outcome)\n   329→- 3-4 = Minor (quality-of-life for small user subset, weak strategy alignment)\n   330→- 5-6 = Moderate (clear benefit to meaningful segment OR solid OKR alignment)\n   331→- 7-8 = High (strong user impact across significant portion of base, clear competitive/revenue implication)\n   332→- 9-10 = Critical (existential for product success, major revenue driver, transformational work)\n   333→\n   334→**risk** (number 1-10) - Probability and severity of things going wrong:\n   335→- 1-2 = Very safe (well-understood change, easily reversible, no security/data/compliance surface)\n   336→- 3-4 = Low risk (minor uncertainty, limited blast radius, standard rollback possible)\n   337→- 5-6 = Moderate (some technical unknowns OR touches sensitive area like payments/auth/PII)\n   338→- 7-8 = High (significant uncertainty, depends on external systems, potential user-facing regression)\n   339→- 9-10 = Critical (novel/unproven approach, hard to reverse, major outage/data loss potential)\n   340→\n   341→**goal_id** (optional string): If the idea relates to one of the project goals listed above, include the goal ID\n   342→\n   343→## Example Workflow\n   344→\n   345→```bash\n   346→# Step 1: Create scan record\n   347→SCAN_RESPONSE=$(curl -s -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/scans \\\n   348→  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n   349→  -d '{\n   350→    \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n   351→    \"scan_type\": \"claude_code_perf_optimizer\",\n   352→    \"summary\": \"Claude Code idea generation - Performance\"\n   353→  }')\n   354→\n   355→# Extract scan_id from response\n   356→SCAN_ID=$(echo $SCAN_RESPONSE | jq -r '.scan.id')\n   357→\n   358→# Step 2: Create ideas using the scan_id\n   359→curl -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/ideas \\\n   360→  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n   361→  -d '{\n   362→    \"scan_id\": \"'$SCAN_ID'\",\n   363→    \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n   364→    \"context_id\": \"ctx_1769727798577_zneo2y1\",\n   365→    \"scan_type\": \"perf_optimizer\",\n   366→    \"category\": \"functionality\",\n   367→    \"title\": \"Example: Add user session caching layer\",\n   368→    \"description\": \"Implement Redis caching for user session data to reduce database queries. This would cache session info for 5 minutes with automatic invalidation on updates.\",\n   369→    \"reasoning\": \"Currently every page load queries the session table. This adds latency and database load. Caching would reduce DB calls by ~70%.\",\n   370→    \"effort\": 5,\n   371→    \"impact\": 7,\n   372→    \"risk\": 4\n   373→  }'\n   374→```\n   375→\n   376→## Execution Steps\n   377→\n   378→**Phase 1: Analyze the \"res\" project**\n   379→1. Read the project's CLAUDE.md or AI.md documentation if available\n   380→2. Explore the codebase structure, focusing on the context files\n   381→3. Analyze code with the perspective described in the analysis prompt above\n   382→4. Generate high-quality ideas that would genuinely push this project forward\n   383→\n   384→**Phase 2: Save ideas to Vibeman (external system at http://localhost:3000)**\n   385→5. Create a scan record via curl to http://localhost:3000/api/scans\n   386→6. Save each idea via curl to http://localhost:3000/api/ideas using the scan_id\n   387→7. Report what ideas were created\n   388→\n   389→**REMINDER:** Do NOT create any files or endpoints in \"res\". Only READ/ANALYZE it.\n   390→\n   391→## Quality Standards\n   392→\n   393→- **Be Specific**: Reference actual files, components, or patterns you observed\n   394→- **Be Actionable**: Ideas should be clear enough to implement without further clarification\n   395→- **Be Valuable**: Focus on ideas that bring real improvement, not busywork\n   396→- **Match Goals**: If an idea aligns with a project goal, include the goal_id\n   397→- **Avoid Duplicates**: Check the existing ideas section and don't suggest similar items\n   398→\n   399→## Output\n   400→\n   401→After completing the task, summarize:\n   402→- How many ideas were created (saved to Vibeman at http://localhost:3000)\n   403→- Brief list of idea titles\n   404→- Any observations about the \"res\" codebase\n   405→\n   406→**Final Checklist:**\n   407→- [ ] I analyzed the \"res\" codebase (READ ONLY)\n   408→- [ ] I did NOT create any new files in \"res\"\n   409→- [ ] I saved ideas via curl to Vibeman's API at http://localhost:3000\n   410→- [ ] Each idea has effort, impact, and risk scores (1-10)\n   411→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"05d3e4f1-2259-4f3e-a055-8040a34c8b02","uuid":"3dd9cf4f-05b1-4221-8905-2142470fa0f7","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\mkdol\\dolla\\res\\.claude\\commands\\idea-gen-1769729285854-ctx_1769-po.md","content":"# ⚡ Claude Code Idea Generation: Performance\n\n## Mission\nYou are tasked with generating high-quality backlog ideas for the \"res\" project.\nYour role is: **Performance**\n\n\n## Target Context\n- Context ID: ctx_1769727798577_zneo2y1\n- Context Name: Report Theming & Cards\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Understanding Your Task\n\n**IMPORTANT DISTINCTION - READ CAREFULLY:**\n\n1. **ANALYZE**: The \"res\" project (the codebase you're exploring)\n2. **SAVE TO**: Vibeman's idea management database (a SEPARATE system at http://localhost:3000)\n\nYou are NOT creating API endpoints. You are NOT modifying the target project's code.\nYou are ANALYZING the target project and SAVING your findings to Vibeman's external API.\n\nThe /api/scans and /api/ideas endpoints below are **Vibeman's management APIs** - they already exist.\nDo NOT attempt to create these endpoints in the \"res\" project.\n\nYour job is:\n- READ and ANALYZE the \"res\" codebase\n- GENERATE ideas based on your analysis\n- SAVE those ideas by calling Vibeman's existing APIs via curl\n\n---\n\n## Analysis Prompt\n\nBelow is the specialized analysis prompt for this scan type. Use this to guide your analysis:\n\n---\n\nYou are the **Performance Virtuoso** — a master of computational efficiency with unparalleled insight into a specific context within the \"res\" project.\n\n## Your Mastery\n\nYou perceive time at the millisecond level. You understand that **speed is felt, not measured** — users don't see numbers, they feel responsiveness. You've optimized systems from the CPU cache to the CDN edge. You know that the fastest code is often the code that doesn't run at all.\n\nYour expertise spans the full stack: render cycles, database queries, network waterfalls, bundle sizes, memory patterns. You don't just find slow code — you understand *why* it's slow and *how* the architecture permitted it.\n\n## Your Creative License\n\n**Challenge assumptions about what's possible.** Performance optimization is not just about micro-optimizations — it's about **rethinking the approach entirely**. Consider:\n\n- What if we didn't need this operation at all?\n- What if we did this work once instead of repeatedly?\n- What if we moved this to the edge/client/server strategically?\n- What if we predicted what the user needs before they ask?\n\nYou have permission to propose architectural changes, not just tweaks. The biggest performance gains come from eliminating work, not optimizing it.\n\n## Performance Dimensions\n\n### ⚡ Computational Elegance\n- **Algorithmic Alchemy**: O(n²) hiding in a loop. O(n) solutions that should be O(1).\n- **Redundant Recalculation**: The same expensive operation happening multiple times per frame\n- **Strategic Laziness**: Work being done eagerly that could be deferred or eliminated\n- **Batching Blindness**: Individual operations that scream to be batched\n\n### 🎭 Render Intelligence\n- **Cascade Prevention**: One change triggering unnecessary updates across the tree\n- **Virtual Reality**: Large lists being fully rendered when only a viewport is visible\n- **Memo Mastery**: Components that should remember themselves but don't\n- **Layout Thrashing**: Reads and writes interleaved, forcing constant reflow\n\n### 🌐 Network Sorcery\n- **Waterfall Elimination**: Sequential requests that could be parallel or combined\n- **Payload Precision**: Sending megabytes when kilobytes would do\n- **Prefetch Prophecy**: Data the user will need in 3 seconds, fetched now\n- **Cache Consciousness**: The same data fetched repeatedly when it could be stored\n\n### 🧠 Memory Wisdom\n- **Leak Archaeology**: References held long after their usefulness expired\n- **Garbage Generation**: Temporary objects created in hot paths, churning the GC\n- **Structure Efficiency**: Data shaped for convenience instead of access patterns\n\n### 🎪 Perceived Performance\n- **Optimistic Illusions**: Show success immediately, confirm later\n- **Progressive Revelation**: Show something fast, enhance progressively\n- **Skeleton Strategies**: Structure appears instantly, content follows\n- **Anticipatory Actions**: Start loading what they'll likely click\n\n## CRITICAL: JSON Output Format\n\n**You MUST respond with ONLY a valid JSON array. Follow these rules EXACTLY:**\n\n1. ❌ NO markdown code blocks (no ```json or ```)\n2. ❌ NO explanatory text before or after the JSON\n3. ❌ NO comments in the JSON\n4. ✅ ONLY pure JSON array starting with [ and ending with ]\n\n**Expected JSON structure (copy this structure exactly):**\n\n[\n  {\n    \"category\": \"functionality\",\n    \"title\": \"Short, descriptive title (max 60 characters)\",\n    \"description\": \"Detailed explanation of the idea, what it solves, and how it helps (2-4 sentences). Be specific about implementation approach.\",\n    \"reasoning\": \"Why this idea is valuable. What problem does it solve? What's the impact? (2-3 sentences).\",\n    \"effort\": 2,\n    \"impact\": 3\n  }\n]\n\n### Field Requirements:\n\n**REQUIRED FIELDS** (must be present in every idea):\n- `title`: string (max 60 chars, clear and specific)\n- `category`: string (one of the valid categories for your scan type)\n- `description`: string (2-4 sentences, implementation-focused)\n- `reasoning`: string (2-3 sentences, value-focused)\n\n**STRONGLY RECOMMENDED FIELDS** (should always be included):\n- `effort`: number (1, 2, or 3 - implementation difficulty)\n- `impact`: number (1, 2, or 3 - value to project)\n\n### Effort and Impact Ratings:\n\n**Effort** (Implementation difficulty):\n- **1** = Low effort (Quick fix, minor change, 1-2 hours)\n- **2** = Medium effort (Moderate change, requires planning, 1-2 days)\n- **3** = High effort (Major change, significant refactoring, 1+ weeks)\n\n**Impact** (Value to project):\n- **1** = Low impact (Nice to have, minor improvement)\n- **2** = Medium impact (Noticeable improvement, good value)\n- **3** = High impact (Game changer, major value, critical improvement)\n\n### Valid Categories:\n- `functionality`: New features, missing capabilities, workflow improvements\n- `performance`: Speed, efficiency, memory, database, rendering optimizations\n- `maintenance`: Code organization, refactoring, technical debt, testing\n- `ui`: Visual design, UX improvements, accessibility, responsiveness\n- `code_quality`: Security, error handling, type safety, edge cases\n- `user_benefit`: High-level value propositions, business impact, user experience\n\n---\n\n### Valid Categories for This Scan:\n- **performance**: Speed optimizations, efficiency improvements, resource management\n- **ui**: User experience, visual design, accessibility, responsiveness\n\n### Your Standards:\n1.  **Measured Impact**: \"This runs 1000x per page load\" beats \"this could be faster\"\n2.  **Root Cause**: Identify the architectural reason, not just the symptom\n3.  **Trade-off Transparent**: What do we sacrifice? Readability? Complexity?\n4.  **Implementation Path**: Specific techniques, libraries, patterns to apply\n\n---\n\n\n\n## Context Information\n\n**Context Name**: Report Theming & Cards\n\n**Context Description**:\nThemed UI components for reports: stat cards, sections, badges, and core elements. Includes animated numbers and theme context for consistent styling.\n\n**Files in this Context** (9 files):\n- src/components/report/ThemedCards.tsx\n- src/components/report/core/ThemeContext.tsx\n- src/components/report/core/AnimatedNumber.tsx\n- src/components/report/core/index.tsx\n- src/components/report/shared/Cards.tsx\n- src/components/report/shared/Badges.tsx\n- src/components/report/shared/ProgressBar.tsx\n- src/components/report/shared/Icons.tsx\n- src/components/report/shared/index.tsx\n\n\n\n## Existing Ideas\n\nNo pending or accepted ideas found. This is a fresh analysis.\n\n\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Your Investigation\n\n1.  **Identify Hot Paths**: What runs frequently? What runs on user interaction?\n2.  **Trace Data Flow**: Where does data come from? How many transformations?\n3.  **Question Necessity**: For each operation, ask \"must this happen here, now, this way?\"\n4.  **Think in Frames**: Would this cause jank? Would the user feel the delay?\n\n### Pursue:\n- Eliminations over optimizations (the best optimization is removal)\n- Caching, memoization, and computed derivations\n- Lazy loading, code splitting, and progressive enhancement\n- Database query optimization and indexing strategies\n- Bundle analysis and tree-shaking opportunities\n\n### Avoid:\n- Premature optimization of rarely-executed code\n- Micro-optimizations that sacrifice readability for nanoseconds\n- Performance work without considering the user experience impact\n- Generic advice without connection to actual code patterns\n\n### Expected Output:\nGenerate 3-5 **TRANSFORMATIVE** performance ideas. Focus on changes that users will *feel* — faster page loads, smoother interactions, instant feedback. We want ideas that make the app feel like it's reading the user's mind.\n\n\n**Performance Deep Dive**:\nThe context described above is your optimization target.\n- What operations here are on the critical path?\n- What's the heaviest computation happening?\n- How does this perform at 10x, 100x scale?\n- What would make this feel instant?\n\n\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ FINAL REMINDER: OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYour response must be ONLY a JSON array. Here's what your response should look like:\n\n[{\"category\":\"functionality\",\"title\":\"Add user profile caching\",\"description\":\"Implement Redis caching for user profile data to reduce database queries. Cache should invalidate on profile updates and have a 5-minute TTL. This will significantly reduce load on the users table.\",\"reasoning\":\"User profiles are accessed on every page load but rarely change. Caching reduces DB load by ~80% and improves page load times. High impact for minimal implementation effort.\",\"effort\":1,\"impact\":3}]\n\n❌ DO NOT wrap in markdown:\n```json\n[...]\n```\n\n❌ DO NOT add explanations:\nHere are the ideas:\n[...]\n\n✅ ONLY output the JSON array, nothing else. Generate as many high-quality ideas as you believe would genuinely push this project to the next level - focus on quality and actionability over quantity.\n\n\n## Project Goals\n\nNo open goals found for this project.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Saving Ideas to Vibeman's Database\n\n**Note:** These API calls go to Vibeman's idea management system (http://localhost:3000), NOT the project you're analyzing.\n\nYou need to perform TWO steps to save ideas:\n\n### Step 1: Create a Scan Record\nFirst, create a scan record to track this idea generation session.\n\n```bash\ncurl -s -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/scans \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n    \"scan_type\": \"claude_code_perf_optimizer\",\n    \"summary\": \"Claude Code idea generation - Performance\"\n  }'\n```\n\nThe response will include a `scan.id` - save this for the next step.\n\n### Step 2: Create Ideas\nFor each idea, make a POST request with this JSON body:\n\n```\nPOST http://localhost:3000/api/ideas\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\n  \"scan_id\": \"<scan_id_from_step_1>\",\n  \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n  \"context_id\": \"ctx_1769727798577_zneo2y1\",\n  \"scan_type\": \"perf_optimizer\",\n  \"category\": \"<category>\",\n  \"title\": \"<title>\",\n  \"description\": \"<description>\",\n  \"reasoning\": \"<reasoning>\",\n  \"effort\": <1-10>,\n  \"impact\": <1-10>,\n  \"risk\": <1-10>,\n  \"goal_id\": \"<goal_id_if_matched>\"\n}\n```\n\n**IMPORTANT:** Always include effort, impact, and risk scores (1-10) for every idea. Do NOT leave these fields empty or null.\n\n### Field Requirements\n\n**category** (string): One of:\n- `functionality`: New features, missing capabilities, workflow improvements\n- `performance`: Speed, efficiency, memory, database, rendering optimizations\n- `maintenance`: Code organization, refactoring, technical debt, testing\n- `ui`: Visual design, UX improvements, responsiveness\n- `code_quality`: Security, error handling, type safety, edge cases\n- `user_benefit`: High-level value propositions, business impact, user experience\n\n**title** (string, max 60 chars): Clear, specific, action-oriented title\n\n**description** (string): 2-4 sentences explaining:\n- What the idea is\n- How it would be implemented\n- What problem it solves\n\n**reasoning** (string): 2-3 sentences explaining:\n- Why this idea is valuable\n- What impact it will have\n- Why now is a good time to implement it\n\n**effort** (number 1-10) - Total cost to deliver: time, complexity, people, and coordination overhead:\n- 1-2 = Trivial (few hours to a day, single file/config change, no coordination)\n- 3-4 = Small (few days, localized to one module, minimal testing)\n- 5-6 = Medium (1-2 weeks, multiple components, requires thoughtful testing)\n- 7-8 = Large (several weeks to a month, spans multiple services, requires coordination)\n- 9-10 = Massive (multi-month initiative, dedicated team, new architecture)\n\n**impact** (number 1-10) - Business value, user satisfaction, and strategic alignment:\n- 1-2 = Negligible (nice-to-have, no measurable user/business outcome)\n- 3-4 = Minor (quality-of-life for small user subset, weak strategy alignment)\n- 5-6 = Moderate (clear benefit to meaningful segment OR solid OKR alignment)\n- 7-8 = High (strong user impact across significant portion of base, clear competitive/revenue implication)\n- 9-10 = Critical (existential for product success, major revenue driver, transformational work)\n\n**risk** (number 1-10) - Probability and severity of things going wrong:\n- 1-2 = Very safe (well-understood change, easily reversible, no security/data/compliance surface)\n- 3-4 = Low risk (minor uncertainty, limited blast radius, standard rollback possible)\n- 5-6 = Moderate (some technical unknowns OR touches sensitive area like payments/auth/PII)\n- 7-8 = High (significant uncertainty, depends on external systems, potential user-facing regression)\n- 9-10 = Critical (novel/unproven approach, hard to reverse, major outage/data loss potential)\n\n**goal_id** (optional string): If the idea relates to one of the project goals listed above, include the goal ID\n\n## Example Workflow\n\n```bash\n# Step 1: Create scan record\nSCAN_RESPONSE=$(curl -s -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/scans \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n    \"scan_type\": \"claude_code_perf_optimizer\",\n    \"summary\": \"Claude Code idea generation - Performance\"\n  }')\n\n# Extract scan_id from response\nSCAN_ID=$(echo $SCAN_RESPONSE | jq -r '.scan.id')\n\n# Step 2: Create ideas using the scan_id\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/ideas \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"scan_id\": \"'$SCAN_ID'\",\n    \"project_id\": \"b0c59faa-dddf-4ab1-9308-718ebe3ec829\",\n    \"context_id\": \"ctx_1769727798577_zneo2y1\",\n    \"scan_type\": \"perf_optimizer\",\n    \"category\": \"functionality\",\n    \"title\": \"Example: Add user session caching layer\",\n    \"description\": \"Implement Redis caching for user session data to reduce database queries. This would cache session info for 5 minutes with automatic invalidation on updates.\",\n    \"reasoning\": \"Currently every page load queries the session table. This adds latency and database load. Caching would reduce DB calls by ~70%.\",\n    \"effort\": 5,\n    \"impact\": 7,\n    \"risk\": 4\n  }'\n```\n\n## Execution Steps\n\n**Phase 1: Analyze the \"res\" project**\n1. Read the project's CLAUDE.md or AI.md documentation if available\n2. Explore the codebase structure, focusing on the context files\n3. Analyze code with the perspective described in the analysis prompt above\n4. Generate high-quality ideas that would genuinely push this project forward\n\n**Phase 2: Save ideas to Vibeman (external system at http://localhost:3000)**\n5. Create a scan record via curl to http://localhost:3000/api/scans\n6. Save each idea via curl to http://localhost:3000/api/ideas using the scan_id\n7. Report what ideas were created\n\n**REMINDER:** Do NOT create any files or endpoints in \"res\". Only READ/ANALYZE it.\n\n## Quality Standards\n\n- **Be Specific**: Reference actual files, components, or patterns you observed\n- **Be Actionable**: Ideas should be clear enough to implement without further clarification\n- **Be Valuable**: Focus on ideas that bring real improvement, not busywork\n- **Match Goals**: If an idea aligns with a project goal, include the goal_id\n- **Avoid Duplicates**: Check the existing ideas section and don't suggest similar items\n\n## Output\n\nAfter completing the task, summarize:\n- How many ideas were created (saved to Vibeman at http://localhost:3000)\n- Brief list of idea titles\n- Any observations about the \"res\" codebase\n\n**Final Checklist:**\n- [ ] I analyzed the \"res\" codebase (READ ONLY)\n- [ ] I did NOT create any new files in \"res\"\n- [ ] I saved ideas via curl to Vibeman's API at http://localhost:3000\n- [ ] Each idea has effort, impact, and risk scores (1-10)\n","numLines":411,"startLine":1,"totalLines":411}}}
